data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing the Prompt's Core**\n\nI'm now carefully analyzing the prompt's constraints. It's in French, posing a complex, multi-faceted question requiring epistemological, mathematical, computational, and interdisciplinary insights focused on searching for rare occurrences. I'm focusing on the interplay between the different fields involved.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 653,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 65,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining the Epistemic Stance**\n\nI've been drilling down into the importance of epistemic honesty. This constraint is critical, especially given my nature. I'm focusing on separating verifiable knowledge from hypothetical assumptions and memory-retrieved citations, and the potential pitfalls of inaccurate recall, and of course, avoiding the fabrication of information or citations. It's a key part of answering the prompt truthfully.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 867,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 279,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Clarifying Knowledge Levels**\n\nI'm now refining my understanding of knowledge levels: \"ce que je sais,\" \"ce que je suppose,\" and \"ce que je cite de mémoire.\" I'm separating verifiable mathematical concepts like theorems and definitions from assumptions, even if my memory of the exact source is fuzzy. I am prioritizing a clear methodological preface to avoid fabricated details or citations. I need to be sure to avoid providing a number without its source.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1127,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 539,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Evidence Categories**\n\nI'm now refining how I classify information: demonstrable mathematical results, memory-retrieved citations (authors, years, empirical data with caveats on accuracy), and assumptions/inferences. I must be precise: no unattributed figures, even in general prose, and be particularly mindful of avoiding the fabrication of information or citations. I'll flag any uncertainty in bibliographic details.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1360,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 772,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Prioritizing Source Verification**\n\nI'm now emphasizing the critical importance of source verification. I must avoid inventing or guessing numerical figures from cited papers. If unsure, I'll stick to qualitative descriptions or only provide a number if I'm entirely certain of its accuracy and source. For instance, I'm verifying the 25/30 statistic from Trinh et al. (2024) for AlphaGeometry, while double-checking the figures for Wu's method. I'm taking extreme care, so I won't provide a number without its source.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1708,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1120,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Verifying Recent Discovery**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a recent breakthrough: FunSearch's discovery of a cap set size of 512 in dimension 8, surpassing the previously known 496. It's $512 = 2^9$, that's right! I'm planning out a rigorous and scholarly response across all six questions, prioritizing accuracy and careful epistemic labeling to ensure a comprehensive and nuanced analysis. I must ensure I adhere to all constraints. I'm carefully organizing the structure to present the solution!\n\n**Deep Planning for Each Question**\n\nI'm establishing a framework for the answer. I'll use explicit tags: \\[Savoir/Démonstration] for verifiable results and \\[Cité de mémoire] for published references. This will ensure clarity and epistemic rigor.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1982,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1394,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Differentiating Information Classes**\n\nI'm now implementing explicit labeling for assumptions and extrapolations: \\[[Supposition/Extrapolation]\\] for hypothetical extensions or experimental propositions. I'll maintain strict adherence to the constraint that no empirical figures are provided without their source. If direct access to the source texts is missing, minor approximations may occur in titles or publication years (pre-print versus journal publication), clearly flagged in case of doubt. I'll also be sure to include a discussion of the processes involved in question one.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2255,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1667,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Recalling NFL's Implications**\n\nI'm focusing on the implications of the No Free Lunch (NFL) theorems. I'm recalling Wolpert and Macready's 1997 paper and its statement of identical performance across all functions. I'm also remembering that NFL doesn't apply to specific function classes, and I'm noting nuances from Igel & Toussaint (2004) and Schumacher, Vose & Whitley (2001). I'm carefully organizing my responses with precise citations and epistemic tags.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2474,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1886,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Search Properties**\n\nI'm now focusing on how specific search strategies perform given certain spaces. I'm recalling that real scientific and computational landscapes aren't uniformly random. In cases of flat fitness landscapes, like those in cryptography or needle-in-a-haystack problems, guided search may underperform random search. I'm starting to think about when guided search offers an advantage, such as in spaces that exhibit smoothness or Lipschitz continuity, where Bayesian Optimization (BO) can be beneficial.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2722,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2134,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining Search Strategy Performance**\n\nI'm now diving into the effectiveness of various search strategies given different landscape characteristics. I'm focusing on the suitability of UCT in hierarchical structures, and how it was analyzed by Kocsis & Szepesvári (2006) and further refined by Coquelin & Munos (2007). I'm also considering evolutionary approaches for problems that benefit from modularity or weak ruggedness, such as unimodal functions or functions with bounded epistasis, and the $O(n \\log n)$ performance vs. random search of $(1+1)$-EA on OneMax (Droste, Jansen & Wegener, 2002).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2989,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2401,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Focusing on Max-Bandits**\n\nI'm now examining max-bandit algorithms, which are crucial for this task. I'm focusing on Carpentier & Valko (2014) and Cicirello & Smith (2005) because I remember them well. Standard bandits are ineffective for heavy-tailed, rare-event spaces; max-bandits are what is required here.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 3276,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2688,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

