data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Publication Challenges**\n\nI'm now focusing on challenges related to published results. I'm noting the issues with unpublished code, raw data, and unstated assumptions (\"tacit knowledge,\" Polanyi 1966; Collins 1974). Also, the lack of incentives for result synthesis and the exploration of consequences, as the pursuit of apparent novelty often discourages thorough investigation and I will integrate it into the model.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 18574,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 17986,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Revising Published Results**\n\nI'm now integrating insights on how technical flaws in informal math/biology (culture conditions, reagents, hidden assumption collapse) affect published results. This includes the 'premature discovery' concept from Stent (1972), such as Avery, MacLeod, and McCarty (1944). I'm carefully organizing this information.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 18863,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 18275,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Estimating Remaining Discoveries**\n\nI'm now focusing on estimating what remains to be discovered, differentiating between counting all objects and finding rare, high-value ones. This involves statistical methods, sequential decision theory, and the complexities of extreme value theory. I am considering the limits of these methods.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 19131,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 18543,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Estimating Unexplored Probability**\n\nI'm now integrating the Good-Turing estimator and its application for estimating the mass of unexplored probability. I'm recalling Good's 1953 work, and also mentioning extensions, like Good-Toulmin from 1956, and Orlitsky's smoothing approach for stability. I will now integrate this into my analysis.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 19465,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 18877,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Estimating Hidden Objects**\n\nI'm now integrating Suresh & Wu (2016) on estimating undiscovered content. Their minimax lower bound, requiring i.i.d. draws and perfect duplicate identification, sets limits on reliable extrapolation. While the Good-Turing estimator focuses on novelty, not high value, guided search violates i.i.d. assumptions. When $f_1 \\approx N$, it offers minimal insight on rare, high-value discoveries, and I will be working on ways to improve this.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 19765,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 19177,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Integrating Capture-Recapture**\n\nI'm now focusing on capture-recapture methods for estimating unexplored space. I'm recalling the Chao1 estimator (Chao, 1984), derived via Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, as a nonparametric lower bound. I will also incorporate the Lincoln-Petersen estimator, generalized to heterogeneous probabilities, and its direct application to multiple generators for estimating the total number of accessible objects. The model depends on the independent nature of the searches.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 20035,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 19447,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Statistical Estimation**\n\nI'm now integrating capture-recapture methods and the biases. I am recalling that estimators, such as the Chao1, require independence and uniform detectability. Violating these assumptions, as in \"easy\" object discovery by multiple generators, leads to systematic underestimation (e.g., Chao 1987). I'm also now considering Bayesian nonparametric processes (Pitman & Yor 1997; Lijoi, Mena & Prünster 2007) for estimating discovery probabilities.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 20328,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 19740,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Estimating the Unseen Value**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on methods for assessing the maximal value remaining, not just novelty. I'm focusing on Extreme Value Theory (EVT) and \"pass@k\" curves, as distinct from the Good-Turing estimator, and am recalling Gnedenko (1943) and Pickands (1975).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 20618,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 20030,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Extreme Value Distributions**\n\nI'm now diving into Extreme Value Theory (EVT) and Generalized Pareto Distributions (GPD). I'm carefully considering how to estimate the shape parameter $\\xi$ and its implications: $\\xi \u003c 0$ implies a finite ceiling, $\\xi = 0$ a logarithmic growth, and $\\xi \u003e 0$ heavy tails with no ceiling. I am assessing the limitations, especially the need for many exceedances, which are sparse when dealing with rare, high-value discoveries.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 20911,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 20323,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Mathematical Constraints**\n\nI'm now incorporating constraints specific to mathematics and optimization. I'm focusing on Lagrangian duality, convex relaxation, and theoretical lower bounds, like Delsarte's bound or semi-definite programming, especially for combinatorial problems such as the cap set. This helps certify optimality gaps, a unique advantage in optimization.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 21210,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 20622,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Formulating Optimal Stopping**\n\nI'm now formulating optimal stopping strategies within a fixed budget B and verification cost c, considering various generators G1...GK. Weitzman's Pandora's Box and the \"one-step lookahead\" rule of Mockus and Jones, Schonlau & Welch, are now at the center of this effort, especially as I work out the expected improvement with fixed budget.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 21486,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 20898,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Optimality Strategies**\n\nI'm now refining optimal stopping. The *one-step lookahead* rule and Weitzman's Pandora's Box are central, especially for budget-constrained expected improvement: $\\text{EI} = \\mathbb{E}[\\max(V_{\\text{next}} - V_{\\text{best}}, 0)] \u003c c$. I'm also remembering Frazier, Powell & Dayanik's work on the knowledge gradient. Further, Rasmussen & Starr's stopping rule is key, and I will be sure to address it.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 21808,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 21220,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Max-Bandit Algorithms**\n\nI'm now integrating insights on max-bandit algorithms, specifically *ExtremeHunter* (Carpentier & Valko, 2014) and *Thresholded Bandits* or *Max-K-Armed Bandits* (Cicirello & Smith, 2005; Streeter & Smith, 2006) for heavy-tailed estimation. I'm also now planning for *Pure Exploration* methods from Audibert, Bubeck & Munos (2010), especially *Successive Halving / Hyperband* (Karnin, Koren & Somekh, 2013; Li et al., 2018) with multi-fidelity verification and detection and switching from saturated generators.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 22132,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 21544,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Hypothesis Verification**\n\nI'm now focusing on hierarchical verification and active screening. I'm recalling the importance of a cheap approximate verifier *h* filtering before an expensive verifier *V*, crucial if *h* has a controlled false-negative rate on high-value rare objects. I'm planning to revisit Wang et al. (2017) and Boudreau et al. (2016) in relation to this challenge.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 22412,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 21824,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Model-Free Falsification**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on question 6, prioritizing model-free falsification. This means constructing verifiable, testable scenarios for each previous response, and ensuring the cost to falsify is minimized. I'm focusing on \"direct measurements\", \"archival data\", and \"randomized controlled experiments\", carefully avoiding all reliance on model opinions or surrogate evaluations. I'll need to carefully examine what would falsify my previous claims.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 22653,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 22065,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Falsification Strategies**\n\nI am now focusing on designing falsification tests for each previous answer. My priority is crafting verifiable scenarios to minimize refutation costs, relying on precise measurements, archival data, or randomized experiments. I'm focusing on the cost of each refutation test. For example, I'm defining a concrete and cheap falsification of answer 1, comparing guided and unguided search in a binary-verification space.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 22865,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 22277,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "2gMDa9HmCp2p5NoPmO3w6A4"}

